# pie — line-ending + diff normalisation
#
# Why this file exists: pie is developed on Windows (x86_64-pc-windows-msvc)
# with `core.autocrlf=true`, but it must build and run on Linux, WSL and macOS.
# Without explicit attributes, a checkout done on one OS differs byte-for-byte
# from a checkout done on another, which makes CI failures unreproducible
# locally (planning/PLATFORMS.md, planning/DESIGN.md §1).
#
# Rules of thumb applied here:
#   * every text file is normalised to LF in the repository (already the
#     convention for the existing tree), and
#   * text files that are consumed by unix tooling are ALSO checked out as LF
#     on every OS, so a Windows checkout matches a Linux checkout exactly.
#
# Attribute precedence: a path-specific rule below always beats
# `core.autocrlf` / `core.eol` from the local or global git config, so pinning
# `eol=lf` here is what makes WSL/Linux behaviour identical to Windows.

# Default: normalise line endings on commit, let git decide on checkout.
* text=auto

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Source, config and script text -> LF on every platform
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Rust sources, manifests and lockfile. LF everywhere: rustc accepts CRLF, but
# a CRLF file trips `cargo fmt --check` (G1) and pollutes diffs, so pin it.
*.rs            text eol=lf
*.toml          text eol=lf
Cargo.lock      text eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF or bash/sh refuse to run them or report
# `bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M` on Linux/WSL/macOS.
*.sh            text eol=lf
.bashrc         text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf
*.zsh           text eol=lf
*.fish          text eol=lf

# CI + other YAML: read by GitHub Actions runners (Linux) as well as by the
# developer on Windows. LF keeps the rendered workflow identical everywhere.
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf

# Data files. `tests/fixtures/*.jsonl` are recorded vendor CLI samples that the
# tolerant line parser (DESIGN §6.4, invariant I5) is tested against; pinning
# LF means the parser sees the same bytes on every OS and the fixtures cannot
# silently change shape between a Windows and a Linux run.
*.json          text eol=lf
*.jsonl         text eol=lf
*.ndjson        text eol=lf

# Documentation + prose.
*.md            text eol=lf
*.txt           text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Windows-native script text -> CRLF on every platform
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PowerShell and cmd.exe do not require CRLF, but some tooling that shells out
# to them (and interactive editing) is markedly less confusing with it. These
# files never run on Linux/WSL/macOS, so CRLF costs nothing there.
*.ps1           text eol=crlf
*.psm1          text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf
*.bat           text eol=crlf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Binary — never normalise, never diff as text
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.exe           binary
*.dll           binary
*.pdb           binary
*.so            binary
*.dylib         binary
*.a             binary
*.lib           binary
*.rlib          binary
*.zip           binary
*.tar           binary
*.gz            binary
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.ico           binary
*.icns          binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
*.ttf           binary

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Linguist / GitHub rendering
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `planning/` is the frozen design + acceptance contract and `knowledge/` is
# reverse-engineered CLI reference, both prose-only Markdown. Marking them as
# documentation keeps them out of the repo's language statistics, which is the
# only classification that is actually true for them.
planning/**     linguist-documentation
knowledge/**    linguist-documentation

# Deliberately NOT marked `linguist-generated`:
# `tests/fixtures/**` are recorded vendor output, but they are byte-exact
# security-relevant samples (secret leakage, I3/I5) that must stay visible in
# pull-request diffs.
